    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d647">

                <pb facs="1171" n="1047" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg1047"/>

                <head>No. 643.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Fairchild to Mr. Evarts.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-01-17"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 17, 1881</date>.
                        (Received February 4.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 106.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your instructions No. 80, of
                    November 12, 1880, I have the honor to inform you that Colonel Mathews, consul
                    at Tangiers, has transmitted to me much new and interesting (and horrible)
                    information regarding the outrageous treatment which the Jews in Morocco
                    continue to receive.</p>
                <p>It seems that the promises of the Sultan have not been kept, and that it is
                    almost impossible to procure the punishment of the officials who are known to be
                    guilty of the crimes of unjustifiably inflicting heavy fines, imprisonment, and
                    severe whippings, even to the death, upon the unhappy victims of their
                    hatred.</p>
                <p>At a meeting of the foreign representatives lately held at Tangier, it was agreed
                    to present to the Sultan’s prime minister, Sid Mohamed Bargash, a collective
                    note of protest against the actions of Moorish officials in this regard.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
                <pb facs="1172" n="1048" xml:id="pg1048"/>
                <p>I suppose the consul at Tangier sends to the Department the same information with
                    which he favors this legation, but, lest I may be mistaken, I inclose a literal
                    copy of the collective note of protest above referred to, as it concisely tells
                    the story of the present situation of affairs in Morocco.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>LUCIUS FAIRCHILD.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d647-a1.xml" xpointer="d647-a1"/>
            </div>
